simusbip runs USB device firmware on a simulated AVR and exports the
device over USB/IP, so a USB/IP client can attach it to a real USB stack.
It is an alternative to extra_board_usb that needs no out-of-tree kernel
module: on Linux the client side is the mainline vhci-hcd driver
(USBIP_VHCI_HCD in the kernel configuration) and the usbip tool,
packaged as usbip, linux-tools or net-misc/usbip depending on the
distribution.

The default firmware is extra_board_usb's at90usb162_cdc_loopback, a CDC
serial port that echoes what it receives.

	make
	./obj-*/simusbip.elf &
	sudo modprobe vhci-hcd
	usbip list -r 127.0.0.1
	sudo usbip attach -r 127.0.0.1 -b 1-1

The device then shows up in lsusb and, for the loopback firmware, as a
/dev/ttyACM device. "sudo usbip port" lists attached devices and
"sudo usbip detach -p <port>" removes one. Stopping simusbip detaches it
too.

Other firmware can be given on the command line, as .hex or .elf. An .elf
with an AVR_MCU section selects the part and clock itself:

	./obj-*/simusbip.elf -m atmega32u4 -f 16000000 firmware.hex
	./obj-*/simusbip.elf ch.elf

simusbip.elf -h lists the options. The flash is kept in simusbip_flash.bin,
so that a bootloader finds the application it was given last time.

When the firmware disconnects from the bus, for instance when a bootloader
starts the application, the USB/IP connection is closed and the device has
to be attached again.

usbip_check.py exercises the server without a kernel: it imports the
device, enumerates it and sends data through the loopback firmware, the
way vhci-hcd and cdc-acm would.

USB/IP: https://docs.kernel.org/usb/usbip_protocol.html
